  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "R(4,k) \\geq c \\, k^{5/2}/(\\log k)^2 for an absolute constant c>0 and all large k, established by the triangle-free process: run to completion it produces (whp) a triangle-free graph G on n vertices with \\alpha(G)=O(\\sqrt{n\\log n}), which converts to a K_4-free coloring of K_m with no blue K_k.",
    "plain": "R(4,k) is the least n forcing a red K_4 or blue K_k in every 2-coloring of K_n. A lower bound R(4,k)>n needs an explicit coloring avoiding both — equivalently a K_4-free graph with independence number below k. Bohman and Keevash build such graphs by a random greedy process (repeatedly add a random edge that keeps the graph triangle-free), which whp yields a triangle-free graph whose largest independent set is only O(\\sqrt{n\\log n}). The goal is to formalize this argument, or a tractable fragment such as the deterministic reduction and single-step expectation estimates, in Lean 4.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Records the best known lower bound on R(4,k), pinning its growth to the window between k^{5/2}/log^2 k and the AKS upper bound k^3/log^2 k.",
      "The triangle-free / self-correcting random process is a flagship modern probabilistic-combinatorics technique never before formalized in a proof assistant.",
      "Extends the parent entry's static first-moment scaffold to a dynamic random process on the lower-bound side."
    ]
  },
